  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Communication is a challenge for 1.8 million Filipinos with hearing difficulty which can prevent them from realising their rights to legal information and processes. Difficulty in accessing legal information, limited availability of sign language interpreters, poor understanding of deaf culture, and lack of accommodations in legal settings can lead to procedural delays and increase the risk of injustice. A 2024 study on legal feminism found that most child abuse cases filed in Philippine courts from 2016 to 2022 involved girls with hearing impairment.
&#x200d;
In 2025, Australia&#x2019;s FAIR Justice facilitated technical advice and consultations to support the Philippine Supreme Court in drafting rules on the use of Filipino Sign Language (FSL) interpreting in judicial proceedings. This support gathered around 100 individuals with hearing impairment, and organisations, judges, court personnel, FSL interpreters, and academics from across the country to ensure that the rules were informed by the perspectives and lived experiences of d/Deaf individuals and advocates.  The rules, once adopted by the Supreme Court, will provide a fairer and more inclusive court experience for d/Deaf Filipinos, with qualified and accredited FSL interpreters available, along with a set of procedures and protocols for engaging them.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In August 2024, Australia&#x2019;s FAIR Justice organised an exchange visit to Australia between the Supreme Court and the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia focused on family dispute resolution. Insights from this visit led to the Rule incorporating provisions on child-inclusive process, and victim and culture sensitivity, and expanding mediator qualifications to include non-lawyers such as social workers and mental health professionals. 
&#x200d;
The Philippine Family Courts Act of 1997 created family courts to handle civil and criminal cases involving families and children. These include annulment, custody, property relations, and crimes involving minors, or related to violence and child abuse.  The breadth and sensitivity of these cases is a challenge for more than 300 family courts in the country. Women and children are often subjected to adversarial legal processes that exacerbate trauma and vulnerability. To address this, the Supreme Court developed and approved the first Rule on Family Mediation in November 2024.  The Rule ensures the availability of trained and court-accredited family mediators, along with mediation procedures to help families resolve conflict outside the courtroom, assist courts in reducing cases, and protect the best interest of the child.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
